PG.notes("l190", "<p class=\"summary\">Defines full-stack development and splits a web app into frontend and backend, with the backend made of server, application and database. Walks through the request/response cycle with a login example and a restaurant comparison.</p>\n<h3>Why full-stack</h3>\n<ul><li>Early startup roles favour people who can build things; job listings for startups mostly ask for full-stack developers.</li><li>Full-stack means handling both the client-facing frontend and the invisible backend that holds the application logic.</li></ul>\n<h3>Frontend vs backend</h3>\n<ul><li>Frontend: everything a user can see, hear or interact with (fonts, colours, buttons, forms).</li><li>Backend: what the user never sees or touches; it makes the app function.</li></ul>\n<h3>The three backend parts</h3>\n<ul><li>Server: a computer that stays on and listens for browser requests. Any networked machine can be a server; during development localhost makes your own computer the server.</li><li>Application: code running on the server that decides how to answer each request. It can return an HTML page, data, or only a status code such as 404, which signals that the request is not something the app knows how to handle.</li><li>Database: persistent storage for user data. Without one, data held only in memory is lost when the machine loses power. Not every app needs one, but larger apps usually do.</li></ul>\n<h3>Website vs web application</h3>\n<ul><li>So far the course built static sites: HTML, CSS and JS files stored somewhere (e.g. GitHub Pages) and returned unchanged on request.</li><li>A web app does more than show pages. Login is the simple example: the browser sends email and password in a request; the server routes it to login logic (e.g. a login.js); the app looks up the email in the database, compares passwords, and responds with the home page on a match or the login page again (with an error) otherwise.</li></ul>\n<h3>Restaurant comparison</h3>\n<ul><li>Diner = client, menu = available interactions, waiter = the request/response carrier, kitchen = server, chef = application with recipes, pantry = database, plated dish = the response (HTML, CSS, JS or data).</li><li>The request/response loop repeats for every new action.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Login flow as runnable JavaScript with an in-memory stand-in for the database (illustrative only; plain-text passwords are for demonstration)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// Stand-in for the database\nconst users = [\n  { email: &quot;a@example.com&quot;, password: &quot;secret123&quot; }\n];\n\n// The 'application' logic the server runs for a login request\nfunction handleLogin(email, password) {\n  const user = users.find(u =&gt; u.email === email); // look up by email\n  if (user &amp;&amp; user.password === password) {\n    return { status: 200, page: &quot;home.html&quot; };     // match: send the home page\n  }\n  return { status: 401, page: &quot;login.html&quot;, error: &quot;Wrong email or password&quot; };\n}\n\nconsole.log(handleLogin(&quot;a@example.com&quot;, &quot;secret123&quot;)); // home.html\nconsole.log(handleLogin(&quot;a@example.com&quot;, &quot;nope&quot;));      // login.html with error</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>A static site hosted on GitHub Pages has no backend logic, so it cannot do login or store data by itself.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Real apps never store or compare plain-text passwords; this is covered in the authentication section.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l191", "<p class=\"summary\">Compares backend languages and frameworks and explains why the course teaches Node.js.</p>\n<h3>Frontend is fixed, backend is open</h3>\n<ul><li>Browsers only run HTML, CSS and JavaScript, so frontend languages are fixed. Frameworks such as React, Angular and Vue are JavaScript libraries that manage the display and client-side logic (e.g. validating an email format).</li><li>On the backend almost any language works, because the only requirement is producing HTML, CSS, JS or data to send back. Common choices: Java, Ruby, PHP, C#, Python, JavaScript.</li></ul>\n<h3>Frameworks by language</h3>\n<ul><li>Ruby: Rails. Java: Spring. C#: ASP.NET. PHP: Laravel, CakePHP. Python: Flask, Django. JavaScript: Node.js (with Express).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo notes list Express, Django, Spring Boot and Rails as typical frameworks.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why Node.js here</h3>\n<ul><li>It reuses the JavaScript already learned for the frontend.</li><li>It tops the Stack Overflow developer survey framework list among professional developers, which means more documentation, community help and job demand.</li><li>Python stays strong for areas like machine learning web apps and C# keeps steady demand, while PHP/Laravel and Ruby/Rails have been declining in popularity.</li><li>The course focuses on one stack rather than surveying all of them.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Language choice on the backend does not change what the browser receives, so avoid agonising over it.</li></ul>");
